\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_markdown_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
# Individual-Level Behavior Dynamics\
\
## Estimating, validating, and interpreting \\(A_i\\), \\(A_i + B_i u_t\\), and \\(A_\{i,c\}\\)\
\
This notebook treats the **individual organism as the primary unit of analysis**.\
\
The central models are:\
\
\\[\
\\text\{Individual pooled operator:\}\\qquad x_\{i,t+1\}=A_i x_\{i,t\}\
\\]\
\
\\[\
\\text\{Individual controlled operator:\}\\qquad\
x_\{i,t+1\}=A_i x_\{i,t\}+B_i u_t+c_i\
\\]\
\
\\[\
\\text\{Individual context-specific operator:\}\\qquad\
x_\{i,t+1\}=A_\{i,c\}x_\{i,t\}\
\\]\
\
where \\(i\\) indexes participant and \\(c\\) indexes experimental phase.\
\
The analysis order is deliberately behavior-analytic:\
\
1. **Can a model trained on earlier behavior of one individual predict later behavior of that same individual?**\
2. What are that individual's eigenvalues and eigenvectors?\
3. Are the individual's eigenvectors stable enough to interpret?\
4. Do similar dynamical modes recur across individuals?\
5. Only then do we summarize across participants.\
\
Cross-participant generalization is therefore secondary rather than the main validation criterion.\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_code_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
from pathlib import Path\
from itertools import combinations\
\
import numpy as np\
import pandas as pd\
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\
\
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\
from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\
from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error, r2_score\
\
pd.set_option("display.max_columns", 100)\
pd.set_option("display.precision", 4)\
\
DATA_PATH = next(\
    (p for p in [Path("events.csv"), Path("/mnt/data/events.csv")] if p.exists()),\
    None\
)\
if DATA_PATH is None:\
    raise FileNotFoundError("Place events.csv beside this notebook.")\
\
raw = pd.read_csv(DATA_PATH).sort_values(\
    ["participant_id", "click_index"]\
).copy()\
\
print(f"Raw clicks: \{len(raw):,\}")\
print(f"Participants: \{raw['participant_id'].nunique()\}")\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_markdown_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
## 1. Reconstruct the behavioral state\
\
We use the same conservative state definition as the prior notebooks:\
\
\\[\
x_t=\
\\begin\{bmatrix\}\
P(A)_t\\\\\
P(\\mathrm\{reward\})_t\\\\\
P(\\mathrm\{switch\})_t\\\\\
\\overline\{\\log(1+\\mathrm\{ICI\})\}_t\
\\end\{bmatrix\}.\
\\]\
\
Each state is a **non-overlapping 10-click bin within phase**.\
\
The corresponding eigenvector entries therefore always mean:\
\
| Position | State feature |\
|---|---|\
| 1 | Choice allocation \\(P(A)\\) |\
| 2 | Obtained reward rate |\
| 3 | Switch rate |\
| 4 | Mean log inter-click interval |\
\
We standardize **within participant**, so an eigenvector describes coordinated change relative to that person's own behavioral distribution.\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_code_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
raw["choice_A_raw"] = (raw["chosen_option"] == "A").astype(int)\
\
raw["switch_raw"] = (\
    raw.groupby("participant_id")["chosen_option"]\
       .transform(lambda s: s.ne(s.shift()).astype(int))\
)\
raw.loc[\
    raw.groupby("participant_id").head(1).index,\
    "switch_raw"\
] = 0\
\
raw["log_ici_for_state"] = np.log1p(\
    raw["ici_s"].clip(lower=0, upper=5)\
)\
\
raw["click_within_phase"] = (\
    raw.groupby(["participant_id", "phase_id"]).cumcount()\
)\
\
BIN_CLICKS = 10\
raw["state_bin"] = raw["click_within_phase"] // BIN_CLICKS\
\
STATE_COLS = [\
    "choice_prop_A",\
    "reward_rate",\
    "switch_rate",\
    "mean_log_ici",\
]\
\
states = (\
    raw.groupby(\
        ["participant_id", "phase_id", "phase_label", "state_bin"],\
        as_index=False\
    )\
    .agg(\
        n_clicks=("click_index", "size"),\
        choice_prop_A=("choice_A_raw", "mean"),\
        reward_rate=("reward_outcome", "mean"),\
        switch_rate=("switch_raw", "mean"),\
        mean_log_ici=("log_ici_for_state", "mean"),\
        bonus_A=("bonus_target", lambda s: np.mean(s == "A")),\
        bonus_B=("bonus_target", lambda s: np.mean(s == "B")),\
    )\
)\
\
states = states.loc[states["n_clicks"] >= 5].copy()\
\
display(\
    states.groupby(["participant_id", "phase_id"])\
          .size()\
          .describe()\
          .to_frame("states_per_participant_phase")\
)\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_markdown_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
## 2. Construct within-phase transitions\
\
For eigenanalysis of a context-specific operator \\(A_\{i,c\}\\), transitions must remain inside the same phase:\
\
\\[\
x_t\\rightarrow x_\{t+1\}.\
\\]\
\
Phase boundaries are excluded here because the goal is to characterize the local dynamics *within* each environment. They can be modeled separately as perturbations.\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_code_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
rows = []\
\
for (pid, phase), g in states.groupby(["participant_id", "phase_id"]):\
    g = g.sort_values("state_bin").reset_index(drop=True)\
\
    for j in range(len(g)-1):\
        if g.loc[j+1, "state_bin"] != g.loc[j, "state_bin"] + 1:\
            continue\
\
        row = \{\
            "participant_id": pid,\
            "phase_id": int(phase),\
            "phase_label": g.loc[j, "phase_label"],\
            "transition_order": j,\
            "bonus_A_next": g.loc[j+1, "bonus_A"],\
            "bonus_B_next": g.loc[j+1, "bonus_B"],\
        \}\
\
        for col in STATE_COLS:\
            row[f"\{col\}_t"] = g.loc[j, col]\
            row[f"\{col\}_next"] = g.loc[j+1, col]\
\
        rows.append(row)\
\
tr = pd.DataFrame(rows)\
\
print(f"Within-phase transitions: \{len(tr):,\}")\
display(\
    tr.groupby("participant_id")\
      .size()\
      .describe()\
      .to_frame("transitions_per_participant")\
)\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_markdown_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
# Part A \'97 Within-individual prediction\
\
## 3. Temporal holdout scheme\
\
For **each participant \'d7 phase**:\
\
- first 70% of transitions = training\
- last 30% = held-out test\
\
This means every participant is evaluated against their **own later behavior**.\
\
The split is performed separately within phase so the training set contains examples from all environments rather than merely training on early phases and testing on a novel late phase.\
\
This is not the only possible validation design. It answers a specific question:\
\
> Once we have observed some of this individual's behavior in each environment, how well does the estimated dynamical architecture predict later behavior from the same individual?\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_code_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
def temporal_masks_for_participant(g, train_fraction=0.70, min_phase_n=5):\
    train = pd.Series(False, index=g.index)\
    test = pd.Series(False, index=g.index)\
\
    for phase, gp in g.groupby("phase_id"):\
        gp = gp.sort_values("transition_order")\
        n = len(gp)\
\
        if n < min_phase_n:\
            continue\
\
        cut = max(2, int(np.floor(train_fraction * n)))\
        cut = min(cut, n-1)\
\
        train.loc[gp.index[:cut]] = True\
        test.loc[gp.index[cut:]] = True\
\
    return train, test\
\
outer_split_rows = []\
\
for pid, g in tr.groupby("participant_id"):\
    train_mask, test_mask = temporal_masks_for_participant(g)\
\
    outer_split_rows.append(\{\
        "participant_id": pid,\
        "n_train": int(train_mask.sum()),\
        "n_test": int(test_mask.sum()),\
        "phases_in_test": int(g.loc[test_mask, "phase_id"].nunique()),\
    \})\
\
split_summary = pd.DataFrame(outer_split_rows)\
display(split_summary.describe(numeric_only=True))\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_markdown_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
## 4. Why regularize individual operators?\
\
A \\(4\\times4\\) matrix contains 16 state-transition coefficients. A participant-specific phase may contain only ~15\'9625 usable transitions.\
\
Ordinary least squares can therefore produce unstable matrices whose eigenvectors change greatly after tiny perturbations.\
\
We use ridge regression:\
\
\\[\
\\hat C\
=\
\\arg\\min_C\
\\left\\\{\
\\|Y-XC\\|^2+\\alpha\\|C\\|^2\
\\right\\\}.\
\\]\
\
With standardized state variables, \\(\\alpha\\) has a reasonably consistent meaning.\
\
Importantly, we **do not choose \\(\\alpha\\) using the held-out test data**. For each participant and each model architecture, \\(\\alpha\\) is selected using an inner temporal validation split inside the outer training set.\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 cells\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 append(\cf11 \strokec11 nbf\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 v4\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 new_code_cell(\cf12 \strokec12 r"""\
ALPHAS = [0.0, 0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0]\
\
def make_inputs(df):\
    return np.column_stack([\
        (df["phase_id"].to_numpy() == 2).astype(float),\
        (df["phase_id"].to_numpy() == 3).astype(float),\
        (df["phase_id"].to_numpy() == 4).astype(float),\
        df["bonus_A_next"].to_numpy(float),\
        df["bonus_B_next"].to_numpy(float),\
    ])\
\
def inner_temporal_split(train_df, fraction=0.75):\
    inner_train = pd.Series(False, index=train_df.index)\
    inner_val = pd.Series(False, index=train_df.index)\
\
    for phase, gp in train_df.groupby("phase_id"):\
        gp = gp.sort_values("transition_order")\
        n = len(gp)\
\
        if n < 4:\
            inner_train.loc[gp.index] = True\
            continue\
\
        cut = max(2, int(np.floor(fraction*n)))\
        cut = min(cut, n-1)\
\
        inner_train.loc[gp.index[:cut]] = True\
        inner_val.loc[gp.index[cut:]] = True\
\
    return inner_train, inner_val\
\
\
def ridge_fit(X, Y, alpha, fit_intercept=True):\
    model = Ridge(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=fit_intercept)\
    model.fit(X, Y)\
    return model\
\
\
def select_alpha(train_df, architecture):\
    """\cf10 \strokec2 \
    \cf12 \strokec12 Choose alpha using only an inner temporal split of this participant'\cf10 \strokec2 s\
    outer\cf8 \strokec8 -\cf11 \strokec11 training\cf10 \strokec2  observations\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 \
    """\
    inner_train_mask, \cf11 \strokec11 inner_val_mask\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 inner_temporal_split\cf10 \strokec2 (\cf11 \strokec11 train_df\cf10 \strokec2 )\
\
    \cf11 \strokec11 inner_train\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 train_df\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 loc[\cf11 \strokec11 inner_train_mask\cf10 \strokec2 ]\
    \cf11 \strokec11 inner_val\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 train_df\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 loc[\cf11 \strokec11 inner_val_mask\cf10 \strokec2 ]\
\
    \cf8 \strokec8 if\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 len\cf10 \strokec2 (\cf11 \strokec11 inner_val\cf10 \strokec2 ) \cf8 \strokec8 ==\cf10 \strokec2  \cf13 \strokec13 0\cf10 \strokec2 :\
        \cf8 \strokec8 return\cf10 \strokec2  \cf13 \strokec13 1.0\cf10 \strokec2 \
\
    Xcols = [\cf12 \strokec12 f"\cf10 \strokec2 \{\cf11 \strokec11 c\cf10 \strokec2 \}\cf12 \strokec12 _t"\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 for\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 c\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 in\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 STATE_COLS\cf10 \strokec2 ]\
    \cf11 \strokec11 Ycols\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  [\cf12 \strokec12 f"\cf10 \strokec2 \{\cf11 \strokec11 c\cf10 \strokec2 \}\cf12 \strokec12 _next"\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 for\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 c\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 in\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 STATE_COLS\cf10 \strokec2 ]\
\
    \cf11 \strokec11 scaler\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 StandardScaler\cf10 \strokec2 ()\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 fit(\cf11 \strokec11 inner_train\cf10 \strokec2 [\cf11 \strokec11 Xcols\cf10 \strokec2 ])\
    \cf11 \strokec11 Xtr\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 scaler\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 transform(\cf11 \strokec11 inner_train\cf10 \strokec2 [\cf11 \strokec11 Xcols\cf10 \strokec2 ])\
    \cf11 \strokec11 Ytr\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 scaler\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 transform(\cf11 \strokec11 inner_train\cf10 \strokec2 [\cf11 \strokec11 Ycols\cf10 \strokec2 ])\
    \cf11 \strokec11 Xva\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 scaler\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 transform(\cf11 \strokec11 inner_val\cf10 \strokec2 [\cf11 \strokec11 Xcols\cf10 \strokec2 ])\
    \cf11 \strokec11 Yva\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 scaler\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 transform(\cf11 \strokec11 inner_val\cf10 \strokec2 [\cf11 \strokec11 Ycols\cf10 \strokec2 ])\
\
    \cf11 \strokec11 scores\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  []\
\
    \cf8 \strokec8 for\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 alpha\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 in\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 ALPHAS\cf10 \strokec2 :\
        \cf8 \strokec8 if\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 architecture\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 ==\cf10 \strokec2  \cf12 \strokec12 "pooled"\cf10 \strokec2 :\
            model = \cf11 \strokec11 ridge_fit\cf10 \strokec2 (\cf11 \strokec11 Xtr\cf10 \strokec2 , \cf11 \strokec11 Ytr\cf10 \strokec2 , \cf11 \strokec11 alpha\cf10 \strokec2 )\
            \cf11 \strokec11 pred\cf10 \strokec2  \cf8 \strokec8 =\cf10 \strokec2  \cf11 \strokec11 model\cf8 \strokec8 .\cf10 \strokec2 predict(\cf11 \strokec11 Xva\cf10 \strokec2 )\
\
        \cf8 \strokec8 elif\cf10 \strokec2  architecture \cf8 \strokec8 ==\cf10 \strokec2  \cf12 \strokec12 "dmdc"\cf10 \strokec2 :\
