# Interpretation framework\
\
This notebook intentionally prevents a pooled eigenvector from being treated as if it described every organism.\
\
A phase-level claim such as:\
\
> "Scarcity is characterized by a reward-rate-dominated mode"\
\
should be supported by **individual-level evidence** of the following form:\
\
1. Individual models predict held-out later behavior better than useful baselines.\
2. A substantial proportion of individuals show reward rate as the largest dominant-mode loading.\
3. Those individual reward-dominated eigenvectors are stable under within-person block bootstrap.\
4. Their spectral radii are themselves reasonably stable.\
5. The pattern is not produced by only a few participants with unusually large leverage.\
\
If those criteria are not met, the correct conclusion is instead:\
\
> The pooled operator contains a reward-dominated direction, but individuals exhibit heterogeneous local dynamics.\
\
That distinction is scientifically important.\
\
---\
\
# Next model: switching controlled dynamics at the individual level\
\
The next logical model is\
\
\\[\
x_\{i,t+1\}\
=\
A_\{i,z_t\}x_\{i,t\}\
+\
B_\{i,z_t\}u_t\
+\
\\epsilon_\{i,t\},\
\\]\
\
where \\(z_t\\) is a latent behavioral mode.\
\
This asks whether an individual's HMM-like states correspond to genuinely different **local laws of motion**, rather than merely different clusters of observed behavior.\
\
The relevant comparison becomes:\
\
\\[\
\\text\{Individual DMDc\}\
\\quad\\text\{vs\}\\quad\
\\text\{Individual HMM\}\
\\quad\\text\{vs\}\\quad\
\\text\{Individual switching DMDc\}.\
\\]\
\
The decisive outcome should again be **prediction of held-out later behavior from the same individual**, followed secondarily by whether recurring dynamical regimes generalize across organisms.\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 ))\
