# Part D \'97 Within-person cross-phase similarity\
\
## 13. Does the same person retain similar modes across environments?\
\
Two comparisons are particularly interesting:\
\
- A-advantage vs. B-advantage: same broad contingency structure, reversed advantaged option.\
- Symmetric vs. Scarcity: pooled analysis suggested a larger dynamical difference.\
"""\cf10 \strokec2 )\
\
\cf11 \strokec11 code\cf10 \strokec2 (\cf12 \strokec12 r'''\
similarity_rows = []\
\
for pid in tr["participant_id"].unique():\
    for p, q in combinations([1,2,3,4], 2):\
        kp = (pid, p)\
        kq = (pid, q)\
\
        if kp not in individual_phase or kq not in individual_phase:\
            continue\
\
        vp = np.asarray(\
            individual_phase[kp]["dominant_vector"], dtype=complex\
        )\
        vq = np.asarray(\
            individual_phase[kq]["dominant_vector"], dtype=complex\
        )\
\
        cosine = (\
            np.abs(np.vdot(vp, vq))\
            / (np.linalg.norm(vp)*np.linalg.norm(vq))\
        )\
\
        similarity_rows.append(\{\
            "participant_id": pid,\
            "phase_1": p,\
            "phase_2": q,\
            "comparison": f"\{p\} vs \{q\}",\
            "cosine": float(np.real(cosine)),\
